## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need Five Yucas. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

## <h2 style="color: #1a5276;">LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Five Yucas.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Discuss learned content from audio lessons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend basic facts about quantities in market settings (e.g., 'How many apples?' 'Three apples, please.'). Can identify key details in talks about prices and items at the market (e.g., 'The apples are one dollar each.'). Can discuss main ideas from simple audio lessons on shopping (e.g., 'We buy yucas (cassava) and bread at the market.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## <h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of various market items (e.g., pineapple, yuca/cassava, potatoes, money). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to shout out item names. Then the teacher asks, "Where do we buy these things?" leading to the word 'market'. Teacher introduces a simple song or chant about going to the market (e.g., "To the market, to the market, what do I need to buy?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary using flashcards and realia (if available): 'market', 'pineapple', 'cassava' (yuca), 'potatoes', 'money', 'buy', 'need', 'want'. The teacher clearly enunciates the /p/ sound in 'pineapple' and /m/ sound in 'money'. The teacher also models simple phrases like, "I need two yucas." and "How much is it?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding of core vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a pineapple a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected answer: A fruit!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we buy things at the market or at school?" (Expected answer: At the market!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I ask 'How much?', am I asking for the quantity or the price?" (Expected answer: The price!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (1-2 minutes, 4-6 exchanges) between a customer and a vendor at a market. The dialogue focuses on buying yucas, pineapples, and potatoes, using 'How much?', 'How many?', 'I need...', 'I want...', and numbers 1-10.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippet (Audio):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: none; margin-left: 0; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Customer: "Excuse me, how much is the pineapple?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vendor: "It's two dollars."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Customer: "Okay. I need one pineapple and five yucas, please."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vendor: "Here you are! That's seven dollars."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can comprehend basic facts about quantities and prices in market settings. Can identify key details in talks about prices and items at the market.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are the two people talking about?" (Expected: Shopping / Buying food). The teacher plays the audio once.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and mark T or F for simple statements. (e.g., "The customer buys apples." / "The pineapple costs one dollar.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a third time and match pictures of items (e.g., yuca, pineapple) with spoken quantities (e.g., 'five', 'one') or prices on a worksheet.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> WH-Questions ('How much?', 'How many?'), Quantities ('I need five potatoes.'), Present Simple ('I want three apples and one banana.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> market, price, shopping list, item, pineapple, cassava (yuca), potatoes, money, buy, need, want, how much, how many, numbers 1-100.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /p/ in 'pineapple', /m/ in 'money'. Blending of spoken simple onsets and rhymes for numbers (e.g., 'one' /w/ /ʌn/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about prices ('How much is it?'), Describing quantities ('I need five apples.'), Discourse marker ('I need two apples and four bananas.').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the audio dialogue transcript but with key words (numbers, item names, 'how much', 'how many', 'dollars') removed. The teacher plays the audio dialogue again (once or twice), and students fill in the missing words. Focus on careful listening for specific details.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Substitution Drill (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher prepares a series of short audio prompts (e.g., "I want three potatoes." / "The yuca is one dollar." / "How many pineapples do you need?"). For each prompt, students listen and circle the correct number or item on a prepared worksheet that has multiple-choice options (e.g., picture of 2, 3, or 5 potatoes; 'one dollar', 'two dollars', 'three dollars').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listen and Draw/Tick):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a "Market Shopping List" handout with pictures of market items (pineapples, yucas, potatoes) and empty boxes for quantity. The teacher dictates a simple shopping list (e.g., "I need two pineapples. I want five yucas. Please get three potatoes."). Students listen carefully and draw the correct quantity of each item or tick the correct number next to the pictures. They then compare their lists with their partner.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Listen and Create My Shopping List"</b> Students will perform a listening comprehension task where they actively process spoken information to create a visual/written output. This directly ties into the theme "I Need Five Yucas."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a new, slightly longer audio recording of a customer giving a shopping list to a cashier, including items, quantities, and simple prices. The recording uses polite expressions (e.g., "Excuse me," "please").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a blank "My Market Shopping List" template. Their task is to listen attentively to the audio and write down or draw the items and their quantities, and if mentioned, the price. For example, if the audio says "I need five yucas and two pineapples," students draw five yucas and two pineapples on their list. If the audio says "The pineapple is three dollars," they write "$3" next to the pineapple.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The audio is played at least twice. Students should focus on capturing all the details.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, students can briefly compare their lists with a neighbor (without speaking, just visually checking) before submitting.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension, accurate identification of items, quantities, and prices from spoken input, and collaborative interaction during comparison.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist during the "Listen and Create My Shopping List" activity, noting students' engagement and their ability to follow spoken instructions. The completed "My Market Shopping List" will be collected and reviewed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their individual "My Market Shopping List" from Stage 4, demonstrating their ability to accurately comprehend and record spoken quantities, items, and prices.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying and listing the correct items from the audio.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying and recording the correct quantities for each item.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional) Accuracy in identifying and recording prices if included in the audio.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of their written/drawn shopping list.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can listen and understand numbers when people talk about items." (Yes / Almost / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can identify common market items when I hear their names." (Yes / Almost / No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review, self-assessment, and future planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> Students reflect on their learning experience with these guiding questions:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to and understand today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the shopping list was difficult for me?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the listening activities worked well? (e.g., audio clarity, student engagement with the task).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future listening lessons or for students who struggled (e.g., slower audio, more repetition, visual support for numbers)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully identify the core vocabulary and quantities when listening?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job listening today! Tomorrow, we will use the same shopping lists and market words to *read* a short story about going to the market. We'll find out what else people buy!" (This sets the stage for Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension, using the same vocabulary.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>